-- Algebra: typeclass abstractions for arithmetic structures.
--
-- Ring is the scalar typeclass — a commutative ring with
-- multiplicative identity. Float satisfies Ring via the prelude's
-- Numeric operations.
--
-- VectorSpace[V, F] requires Ring[F] — a vector space over the
-- scalar field F. Vec3 (in anthill.geometry) provides
-- VectorSpace[V = Vec3, F = Float].
--
-- This is a more abstract layer than anthill.prelude.Numeric (which
-- bundles the arithmetic categories + Ord into one spec for primitive
-- arithmetic types). Ring is intentionally Ord-free so non-ordered fields
-- (complex numbers, finite fields) can satisfy it later if needed.

namespace anthill.prelude.algebra

  -- =================================================================
  -- Ring — commutative ring with multiplicative identity
  -- =================================================================
  --
  -- Operations are total. Subtraction is defined (a - b = a + (-b),
  -- with the additive inverse implicit). Division is NOT — that's a
  -- Field, a separate spec we'll add when needed.
  --
  -- Float satisfies Ring via the prelude's Numeric ops + the
  -- multiplicative identity 1.0.
  --
  -- WI-20260825-1WBZT — IT DECLARES NO OPERATION ANY MORE, and the duplication that
  -- removes was live rather than cosmetic. `Ring.add` and `Numeric.add` were two
  -- DIFFERENT operations under one spelling, so a bare `add` in scope of both was
  -- "member of sorts Numeric, Ring, not in scope as a bare name here" — resolved only
  -- because the implicit tier deterministically answered `Numeric.add`. Worse, the
  -- additive identity had two NAMES (`Ring.zero` and `Numeric.zero-val`), which do not
  -- collide, so a carrier providing both specs got two `sort_ops` entries for one
  -- concept and nothing complained. One declaration per short name is the fix, and
  -- `provides` is how this sort reaches it (`arithmetic.anthill`'s header, and the
  -- `Eq provides PartialEq` precedent WI-1110 measured).
  --
  -- `Float` NOW REACHES `Additive` BY TWO PATHS — `provides Numeric[T = Float]` and
  -- `provides Ring[Float]` are both written in `anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill` — and
  -- that diamond is the BENIGN one: the base declares each operation ONCE and both
  -- branches only PROVIDE, so there is no "which parent's method" question for a merge to
  -- get wrong. Driven on user sorts (that shape loads clean, and implementation stays
  -- CARRIER-directed — each route resolves to the carrier's own member by the short-name
  -- join); for `Float` itself the resolution bottoms out in the host builtin registered
  -- on the spec op, since `Float` declares no `add` of its own — which is separately
  -- WI-944's subject, `fact Ring[Float]` being an unbacked provision.
  --
  -- What keeps the diamond benign is the declare-once discipline itself, and that is
  -- enforced NOWHERE: a spec redeclaring a provided base's operation loads clean, and
  -- with two such specs over one carrier the answer follows SOURCE ORDER. WI-20260825-EBMG8
  -- owns that gap; this sort is the shape it has to keep safe.
  sort Ring
    sort T = ?

    provides Additive[T = T]
    provides Multiplicative[T = T]

    -- WHAT IS LEFT IS WHAT IS GENUINELY RING'S. `add_comm` / `add_assoc` /
    -- `add_identity` moved to `Additive` and `mul_assoc` / `mul_identity` to
    -- `Multiplicative` — each sits with the operation that declares it. These two do
    -- not: `distrib` reads BOTH operations, so no single category can state it, and
    -- `mul_comm` is a claim about multiplication that is false in general (matrices,
    -- quaternions) and true of the COMMUTATIVE ring this sort names itself after.
    rule {
      mul_comm:      mul(?a, ?b) <=> mul(?b, ?a)
      distrib:       mul(?a, add(?b, ?c)) <=> add(mul(?a, ?b), mul(?a, ?c))
    }
  end

  -- =================================================================
  -- VectorSpace[V, F] — V is a vector space over the field F
  -- =================================================================
  --
  -- Two type parameters: V (the vector type) and F (the scalar type).
  -- F must be a ring (we don't require multiplicative inverses — a
  -- module over a ring is sufficient for the geometric content; the
  -- name is "VectorSpace" by convention but technically the spec is
  -- a left-module).
  --
  -- Vec3 (in anthill.geometry) provides VectorSpace[V = Vec3, F = Float]
  -- via per-component implementations.
  sort VectorSpace
    -- The scalar-side categories the laws below name. WRITTEN OUT rather than left to the
    -- `requires Ring[F]` chain, because an import is a CHECKED position and the laws are
    -- not: measured on scaland, loading `algebra.anthill` without `arithmetic.anthill`
    -- makes this line say "unresolved name 'Additive' in scope 'anthill.prelude'", while
    -- `Ring`'s own `provides Additive[T = T]` says NOTHING — an unresolved provision
    -- target degrades to an interned name. So this import is what turns "the categories
    -- are in scope here" from an assumption into something the loader states.
    --
    -- A SIBLING `import anthill.prelude.algebra.{Ring}` STOOD HERE TOO and is gone:
    -- `Ring` gaining a cross-namespace `provides` had made a bare `Ring` ambiguous
    -- against a user's top-level `sort Ring`, and that was WI-20260825-N2865, fixed in
    -- the resolver rather than worked around here.
    import anthill.prelude.{Additive, Multiplicative}

    sort V = ?
    sort F = ?
    requires Ring[F]

    operation {
      vec_add(a: V, b: V) -> V        -- vector addition
      vec_sub(a: V, b: V) -> V        -- vector subtraction
      vec_scale(c: F, v: V) -> V      -- scalar multiplication
      vec_zero() -> V                  -- additive identity vector
    }

    -- Scalar-side ops are explicitly qualified in the laws below, and WI-20260825-1WBZT
    -- CHANGED WHICH PREFIX IS CORRECT. They read `Ring.add` / `Ring.sub` / `Ring.mul` /
    -- `Ring.zero` / `Ring.one`, on the ground that "the same names are also in scope from
    -- `anthill.prelude.Numeric` and the resolver can't disambiguate without the prefix".
    -- That reason is GONE — the categories declare each short name ONCE, so there is
    -- nothing left to disambiguate — and at the time those five addresses named NOTHING:
    -- `Ring` reaches its operations by `provides` and declares none of them.
    --
    -- NOTHING CAUGHT IT, which is why this note is long. An equational law's head and RHS
    -- are UNCHECKED positions: driven on the built tree, `rule r: f(?a) <=> Bogus.nope(?a)`
    -- inside a sort loads clean, byte-identically to the `Ring.sub` spelling — so "the
    -- stdlib loads" distinguishes a live address from a typo not at all. The same name in
    -- a rule-body GOAL is two loud errors ("rule-body term `Ring.sub` names nothing")
    -- and in an operation body one ("unknown functor"); only the law position is silent.
    -- Filed as WI-20260825-6RRVA; `wi_1wbzt_syntax_category_test::
    -- the_scalar_side_law_addresses_are_live_and_a_dead_one_is_still_loud` is the row that
    -- pins these five through the guarded position instead.
    --
    -- `Ring.sub` RESOLVES AGAIN SINCE WI-20260825-X9RRN — to `Additive.sub`, through the
    -- `provides` above — so the spelling below is now a CHOICE rather than a repair, and
    -- the reason to keep it is that it names the sort that DECLARES each operation. That
    -- is the same reason the import on the next line is written out: one address per
    -- declaration is what the category split bought, and a law is the last place to spend
    -- it. The row above still measures the difference, because it asserts which SYMBOL
    -- each address lands on rather than that it loads.
    rule {
      vec_add_comm:        vec_add(?a, ?b) <=> vec_add(?b, ?a)
      vec_add_assoc:       vec_add(vec_add(?a, ?b), ?c) <=> vec_add(?a, vec_add(?b, ?c))
      vec_add_identity:    vec_add(?a, vec_zero) <=> ?a
      vec_sub_def:         vec_sub(?a, ?b) <=> vec_add(?a, vec_scale(Additive.sub(Additive.zero, Multiplicative.one), ?b))
      vec_scale_identity:  vec_scale(Multiplicative.one, ?v) <=> ?v
      vec_scale_assoc:     vec_scale(?c, vec_scale(?d, ?v)) <=> vec_scale(Multiplicative.mul(?c, ?d), ?v)
      vec_scale_distrib_v: vec_scale(?c, vec_add(?a, ?b)) <=> vec_add(vec_scale(?c, ?a), vec_scale(?c, ?b))
      vec_scale_distrib_s: vec_scale(Additive.add(?c, ?d), ?v) <=> vec_add(vec_scale(?c, ?v), vec_scale(?d, ?v))
    }
  end
end
